    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-country national-number length rule in the telephone normalization logic, which is the single source of truth used by both the request-time `@TelephoneValidation` (which produces a 400) and the mapper/persistence path.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`:\n- `normalizeTelephone` now rejects (returns `null`) a number whose national number (the digits after the country code) doesn't match the length its country code requires: `+61` requires 9 national digits, `+1` requires 10.\n- Added a small `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS` table (longest country code first for unambiguous prefix matching) and a `hasValidNationalNumberLength` helper. Unrecognized country codes keep the existing generic 8\u201315 digit bound with no extra constraint.\n- Updated the Javadoc to document the new rule.\n\nBecause the validator delegates to `normalizeTelephone`, a wrong-length number like `+61 123` (3 national digits) now fails Bean Validation and the endpoint returns **400**, satisfying the new `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` acceptance test. The previously-valid `ownerNode()` payload (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes, as does the `+1` + 10-digit NANP form.\n\nTest suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
